# Upstream's own ignore file, kept. `src/.gitignore` ships in the release
# tarball and is reproduced here verbatim, because upstream's conventions are
# upstream's business and none of them is removed to make room for a
# fork-owned entry.
#
# ONE CHANGE, and it is a scoping change rather than a removal. Four entries in
# the upstream file are written as if the file lived in the repository root:
# `/zstd`, `zstdmt`, `bin/` and `build-*`. Upstream's file is at `src/`, so
# those anchored forms mean upstream's own build outputs inside `src/`. Left
# unanchored at the root of *this* repository they would mean something else --
# and `/zstd` at this root is this package's own source directory, which is the
# one path in the tree that must never be ignored. They are re-anchored to
# `src/` in the block below, which keeps each convention and fixes what it
# points at.
#
# THE OTHER THING THIS FILE HAS TO DO: upstream's list contains `*.zst` and
# `tmp*`. Eleven of this package's fixtures are `.zst` files under `fixtures/`,
# and they are the oracle the interoperation tests rest on. A fork-owned
# re-inclusion block appears at the end, and it is not optional.

# --- upstream src/.gitignore, verbatim ---

# Object files
*.o
*.ko
*.dSYM

# Libraries
*.lib
*.a

# Shared objects (inc. Windows DLLs)
*.dll
*.so
*.so.*
*.dylib
*.framework
*.xcframework

# Executables
/zstd
zstdmt
*.exe
*.out
*.app

# Test artefacts
tmp*
*.zst
*.zstd
dictionary.
dictionary
NUL
cmakebuild/
install/

# Build artefacts
contrib/linux-kernel/linux/
projects/
bin/
.buckd/
buck-out/
build-*
*.gcda

# IDE
.clang_complete
compile_flags.txt
*.clang-format

# Other files
.directory
_codelite/
_zstdbench/
*.idea
*.swp
.DS_Store
googletest/
*.d
*.vscode
*.code-workspace
compile_commands.json
.clangd
perf.data
perf.data.old

# --- upstream's entries re-anchored to the tree they describe ---
#
# Each of these is the same convention as the entry above it, pointed at `src/`
# where upstream's own file was already pointing. Nothing is dropped.

/src/zstd
/src/zstdmt
/src/contrib/linux-kernel/linux/
/src/projects/
/src/bin/

# --- zig build artefacts (fork addition) ---
#
# These are directories rather than single files because a normal `zig build`
# creates them, and a normal `zig build` must not dirty the tree or leak into
# the package published from it. None of them is named in build.zig.zon
# `.paths`, so an ignored directory can never become part of the package.
.zig-cache/
zig-out/
# Zig 0.16 fetches dependencies into a project-local directory. This package
# declares no dependencies, so this exists only so that a future one cannot
# commit a fetched tree.
zig-pkg/

# The consumer package builds inside this repository and has its own cache and
# output tree. It is ignored here as well as in consumer/.gitignore so a build
# run from the repository root cannot commit it by accident.
consumer/.zig-cache/
consumer/zig-out/
consumer/zig-pkg/

# --- fork re-inclusions (fork addition) ---
#
# Upstream's `*.zst`, `tmp*`, `*.a`, `*.so` and `build-*` patterns above are
# correct for upstream's build outputs and wrong for this repository's fixture
# set, which is data, not output. These lines re-include the fork's own paths.
# Git applies later rules over earlier ones, so they win, and a rule here
# re-includes a file only because the *file* is matched -- none of the directory
# names below is excluded by any pattern above, so re-inclusion is effective.
#
# The eleven `.zst` fixtures are the concrete reason. They are the frames the
# RFC 8878 tests decode and the frames the CLI and Python binding produced, and
# `fixtures/SHA256SUMS` digests all of them: an ignored fixture is a missing
# fixture, and `@embedFile` on a missing file is a build failure that looks like
# a bug in the build graph rather than a mistake in an ignore file.
!fixtures/
!fixtures/**
!fixtures/**/*.zst
!fixtures/**/*.plain
!fixtures/**/*.txt
!fixtures/SHA256SUMS
!fixtures/PROVENANCE.md

# `smoke.zig` writes one frame into the working directory, reads it back and
# deletes it with a `defer`. The entry is here so that a run interrupted between
# the write and the delete cannot leave a stray frame behind.
zstd-smoke-*.zst

# --- Nix and devenv working-tree artefacts (fork addition) ---
#
# `nix build` writes `result` (and `result-1`, `result-2`, ... on a rebuild)
# into the directory that asked for it, and `devenv` writes its resolved inputs
# and its state beside the project. None of them is named in build.zig.zon
# `.paths`, so an ignored path can never become part of the package either.
# `devenv.lock` and `flake.lock` are deliberately *not* here: they are real
# inputs, they belong to the driver, and ignoring them would be the wrong way to
# keep a build out of a commit.
result
result-*
.devenv/
.devenv.flake.nix
.devenv.flakes/
devenv.local.nix
.direnv/